    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.get_similar_neighbors - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.get_similar_neighbors.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.get_similar_neighbors()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Get neighbors with similar attribute values.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.get_similar_neighbors()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Get neighbors with similar attribute values.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.get_similar_neighbors()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Get neighbors with similar attribute values.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nGet neighbors with similar attribute values.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L29467-L29650){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.get_similar_neighbors(\n    node,\n    node_attr,\n    tol_abs=None,\n    tol_pct=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Similar neighbors](../assets/graphs/get-similar-neighbors.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Node E is outlined in the accent color and nodes B through G carry badges with their value of x. Of E's neighbors, C and F match E's value of 3 and are filled in accent; D and G differ, and B matches but is not a neighbor, so it is faded.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nLooks at the direct neighbors of a starting node and returns those whose numeric\nattribute value falls inside a window around the starting node's own value. The window\ncan be set as an absolute offset (`tol_abs=`), as a percentage of the starting value\n(`tol_pct=`), or left unset for exact matching. Both tolerances take a\n`(below, above)` pair, so the window can be asymmetric.\n\nThis is useful for:\n\n- finding homophilous ties, e.g., friends of similar age or trading partners of similar\n  size\n- checking whether a node's value is in line with its local neighborhood (a node with no\n  similar neighbors may be an outlier)\n- seeding local, attribute-aware expansions such as region growing on spatial graphs\n\nNeighbors are taken with `mode=\"all\"`, so in a directed graph both predecessors and\nsuccessors are considered. Only one hop is examined and the selection is not changed.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[node]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The starting node, given as an integer node ID or a string label. Its value of\n    `node_attr=` sets the center of the similarity window. An unknown ID or label raises\n    `ValueError`.\n\n<code>[node_attr]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Name of the node attribute to compare. The starting node's value must be numeric (an\n    `int` or `float`), otherwise `ValueError` is raised. If the starting node does not\n    have this attribute at all, the result is an empty list. Neighbors that lack the\n    attribute, or whose value is not numeric, are silently skipped.\n\n<code>[tol_abs]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [tuple[float, float] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Absolute tolerance as a `(lower, upper)` tuple of non-negative offsets. With a\n    starting value `x`, neighbors whose value lies in `[x - lower, x + upper]` (inclusive)\n    are returned. For example, `(1.0, 2.0)` accepts values from `x - 1.0` to `x + 2.0`.\n    Defaults to `None`. Give at most one of `tol_abs=` and `tol_pct=`.\n\n<code>[tol_pct]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [tuple[float, float] | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Percentage tolerance as a `(lower_pct, upper_pct)` tuple, expressed in percent (so\n    `10` means 10%) of the magnitude of `x`. Neighbors whose value lies in\n    `[x - |x| * lower_pct / 100, x + |x| * upper_pct / 100]` are returned. For example,\n    `(10, 20)` accepts values from `0.9 * x` to `1.2 * x` when `x` is positive, and from\n    `-110` to `-80` when `x` is `-100`. Defaults to `None`. Because the window scales with\n    `x`, it is suited to attributes that span several orders of magnitude, but it\n    collapses to exact matching when `x` is `0`.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Node IDs of the neighbors whose attribute value falls within the window, in the order\n    returned by `get_neighbors()`. Empty if the starting node lacks the attribute, has no\n    neighbors, or no neighbor is in range. When neither `tol_abs=` nor `tol_pct=` is set,\n    only neighbors with exactly the same value are returned.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the starting node's value for `node_attr=` exists but is not numeric (`int` or\n    `float`), if `node=` is an ID or label not found in the graph, if both `tol_abs=` and\n    `tol_pct=` are given, or if a tolerance offset is negative.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nLet $x_u$ be the starting node's value. With `tol_abs=(a, b)`, a neighbor $v$ is\nsimilar when $x_u - a \\le x_v \\le x_u + b$. With `tol_pct=(p, q)` it is similar when\n$x_u - |x_u| p/100 \\le x_v \\le x_u + |x_u| q/100$. With neither, the condition reduces to\n$x_v = x_u$. Bounds are inclusive on both sides. Python `bool` values are a subclass of\n`int` and are therefore treated as numeric (`True == 1`).\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~get_neighbors`) \n\n:   Every neighbor of a node, whatever its attribute values.\n\n[](`~get_node_attrs`) \n\n:   The values of an attribute for every node.\n\n[](`~assortativity`) \n\n:   Whether neighbors have similar values across the whole graph.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nOf \"A\"'s neighbors, only \"B\" has a `value` within 1 of A's `5.0`:\n\n::: {#7c2499c8 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph()\ngraph.add_nodes(\n    [(\"A\", {\"value\": 5.0}), (\"B\", {\"value\": 5.5}), (\"C\", {\"value\": 10.0})]\n)\ngraph.add_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"A\", \"C\")])\n\nsimilar = graph.get_similar_neighbors(\"A\", \"value\", tol_abs=(1, 1))\n[graph.get_label_from_id(n) for n in similar]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n['B']\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Neighbors of a similar size\n\nTwelve towns are joined by the roads between them, and each has a population. Asking which\nneighbors are within 25% of a town's size finds two pairs. Bramford and Halston find each\nother, but Kestrel Bay finds Greyford, and Greyford doesn't find Kestrel Bay:\n\n::: {#b1f6a60c .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ntowns = ec.towns()\nname = towns.get_label_from_id\n\ndef similar(town):\n    found = towns.get_similar_neighbors(\n        town, \"population\", tol_pct=(25, 25)\n    )\n    return [name(n) for n in found]\n\nchecked = [\"Bramford\", \"Halston\", \"Kestrel Bay\", \"Greyford\", \"Calder\"]\n{town: similar(town) for town in checked}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n{'Bramford': ['Halston'],\n 'Halston': ['Bramford'],\n 'Kestrel Bay': ['Greyford'],\n 'Greyford': [],\n 'Calder': []}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe window is a percentage of the starting town's own population. Greyford's 15,800 is 75%\nof Kestrel Bay's 21,000, inside its window, but Kestrel Bay is 133% of Greyford, outside\nGreyford's. Calder, the largest town at 54,000, has no neighbor anywhere near its size.\n\nSized by population, with the roads between the similar pairs highlighted:\n\n::: {#e10f833d .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ntowns.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\",\n    size_by=\"population\",\n    label_by=\"label\",\n    highlight_edges=[\n        (\"Bramford\", \"Halston\"), (\"Kestrel Bay\", \"Greyford\")\n    ],\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"